      "writtenOn": "2026-10-15T17:19:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Using `.bInterfaceClass \u003d USB_CLASS_VENDOR_SPEC (0xFF)`, `.bInterfaceSubClass \u003d 0x50 (`USB_SUBCLASS_GOOGLE_SERIAL`)`, `.bInterfaceProtocol \u003d 0x01 (`USB_PROTOCOL_GOOGLE_SERIAL`)` causes the Linux kernel `usb_simple_serial` driver on the host labstation to automatically match Google VID `0x18d1` + `0xFF/0x50/0x01` and bind to Interface 1 as a `/dev/ttyUSBx` serial port.\n\nIf `udev`, `ModemManager`, or `servod`\u0027s serial port discovery opens that `/dev/ttyUSBx` node, the kernel submits Bulk IN URBs on `EP2 IN` (`0x82`) and steals `SERVO_FRAME_MSC_REQ` packets from `UsbMscInterface`. Notice that `USB_IFACE_BRIDGE` right below (lines 104-106) uses `0xFF / 0x00 / 0x00` specifically to prevent `usb_simple_serial` from binding—`USB_IFACE_MSC` should also avoid `0x50 / 0x01` (e.g. use `0xFF / 0x00 / 0x00` or a dedicated non-serial vendor subclass).",
